    distGuideTitle: "INFO ON DISTRIBUTIONS",
    distGuideContent: "### Statistical Models\nChoosing the right model is critical. For non-normal data, the app uses the ISO 22514-2 percentile method.\n\n#### **1. Normal Distribution (Gauss)**\nMost common in industry. Assumes data is symmetric around the mean.\n*   **Function**: f(x) = [1 / (σ√(2π))] * e^(-0.5 * ((x-μ)/σ)²)\n\n#### **2. Folded Normal**\nUsed for measures with a natural limit at zero (e.g., runout, flatness).\n\n#### **3. Log-Normal**\nUsed for skewed data with a long tail (e.g., cycle times, particle sizes).\n\n#### **4. Rayleigh**\nRadial distribution for distances from a target in 2D (X and Y).\n*   **Formula**: r = √(x² + y²)\n\n#### **5. Weibull**\nFlexible distribution used for reliability and component lifetime modeling.",
    formulaGuideTitle: "STANDARDS & FORMULAS",
    formulaGuideIntro: "### Calculation Methodology & Standards\n\n#### **1. AIAG / IATF**\nGlobal automotive standard. Defines spread as **6 x sigma (±3σ)**.\n*   **Cp/Cpk**: Uses *Sigma Within*.\n*   **Pp/Ppk**: Uses *Sigma Total*.\n\n#### **2. VDA Band 4**\nGerman automotive standard. Focus on machine verification.\n*   **Percentile Method**: Uses 99.865% percentile (X_0.99865 - X_0.00135) for non-normal data.\n\n#### **3. SEMI**\nStandard for extreme precision in semiconductor manufacturing.\n*   **Cpm (Taguchi)**: Focuses on deviation from Target.\n*   **Cpm Formula**: (USL - LSL) / (6 * √(s² + (Mean - Target)²))",
